Reviewed actuator.hpp. Real concerns below, ordered by severity.

  Bugs

  actuator::reset() won't compile if called — actuator.hpp:107-110
  void reset() { actions.reset(); }
  std::list has no reset(). Should be actions.clear() (and probably
  mapActions.clear() and results.clear() too). Currently a latent
  landmine — only blows up when someone first calls it.

  bind(shared_ptr, method) captures the shared_ptr by reference —
  actuator.hpp:363-378
  return [&obj, method](auto&&... args) mutable -> ... {
    if (obj) { ... }
    else { throw invalid_action(...); }
  };
  The lambda outlives bind's parameter. The only reason this works in
  your tests is that the caller keeps the named shared_ptr variable alive
   in the same scope. Any of these will silently dangle:
  - bind(std::make_shared<T>(), &T::m) — temporary dies immediately
  - auto a = bind(makePtr(), &T::m); — return-value shared_ptr dies at
  the end of the full expression
  - Returning the action from a function

  The obj.reset() test in test_invalid_action only works because of the
  reference capture (capture-by-value would never observe the reset). So
  the design is intentional but fragile. Two safer options:
  1. Capture by value ([obj=obj, method]) — but then reset() from outside
   won't be observed; you'd need a weak_ptr capture instead.
  2. Capture std::weak_ptr by value and lock() inside the lambda. This is
   the conventional safe pattern and preserves the dead-binding
  semantics.

  operator= self-assignment wipes everything — actuator.hpp:98-105
  actions.clear(); mapActions.clear();
  actions = other.actions; mapActions = other.mapActions;
  If &other == this, you clear, then copy from the just-cleared self →
  empty actuator. Add if (this == &other) return *this;.

  operator() mutates caller-owned std::functions through the stored
  pointer — actuator.hpp:139-143
  catch (const invalid_action& ia) { ... *action = nullptr; }
  The actuator stores actionT* to objects the caller owns; on a dead
  binding it nulls the caller's std::function. Surprising side-effect —
  the caller may still be holding action1 and now finds it empty.
  invokeAction instead just erases from the map (actuator.hpp:172) and
  leaves the caller's function intact — inconsistent.

  Design concerns

  invalid_action privately inherits std::exception — actuator.hpp:31
  Private inheritance means catch (const std::exception&) will not catch
  it. And the public data member std::string what shadows the virtual
  std::exception::what() (different signature, same name) — confusing for
   anyone calling e.what(). Either inherit publicly and override what()
  const noexcept, or don't inherit at all.

  Raw actionT* to caller-owned std::functions — actuator.hpp:59,287
  The whole lifetime model relies on the caller keeping every action
  object alive for the actuator's lifetime. There's no enforcement.
  Storing std::function by value (and using indices/IDs for removal)
  would be much safer; the equality-comparison limitation cited in the
  comment is solvable with a handle/token.

  Partial SFINAE → if constexpr migration
  operator() uses if constexpr (modernized in commit 47ee6db), but
  invokeAction still calls select_actuate with enable_if_t overloads. The
   non-void select_actuate (actuator.hpp:261-266) also returns a
  default-constructed T::result_type for no reason — the caller discards
  it. Finish the migration and drop select_actuate.

  return std::move(local) in both connect overloads —
  actuator.hpp:294,323
  Disables NRVO. Just return actuator;.

  Missing const on observers — actuator.hpp:87,235,244
  type(), is_connected(), and has_action() should be const (and
  has_action should take const std::string&). Currently you can't call
  any of them on a const actuator&.

  Minor

  - mapActions doc comment says "Actions list" (actuator.hpp:71) — it's a
   map.
  - mutable on the bind lambdas (actuator.hpp:366,397) is unnecessary —
  nothing in the body mutates a capture.
  - resultsT for void actions resolves to std::vector<int>
  (actuator.hpp:61,68). Harmless because nothing pushes into it on the
  void path, but it's wasted storage on every actuator instance.
  - bind(classT*, method) (actuator.hpp:394) asserts non-null but does no
   runtime check inside the lambda — documented as unsafe, but a nullptr
  check throwing invalid_action would make the two bind overloads
  behaviorally consistent.

    Fix plan for actuator.hpp
    --------------------------

    Bugs (correctness)

    1. Fix reset() — replace actions.reset(); with actions.clear(); mapActions.clear();
    results.clear(); so the method actually compiles and does what its name implies.
    2. Stop dangling reference in bind(shared_ptr, method) — change capture from [&obj, method]
    to a std::weak_ptr captured by value: [wobj = std::weak_ptr<classT>(obj), method](auto&&...
    args) -> ... { if (auto p = wobj.lock()) return ((*p).*method)(...); else throw
    invalid_action(...); }. Drop the unnecessary mutable. This preserves the obj.reset() →
    invalid_action semantics that test_invalid_action relies on, while making it safe to bind
    from temporaries.
    3. Self-assignment guard in operator= — add if (this == &other) return *this; at the top
    before the clear() calls. Also clear results for consistency, or leave it untouched (pick
    one — current code is half-done).
    4. Stop mutating caller-owned std::function from operator()'s catch — drop *action =
    nullptr;. Track dead actions locally (e.g. collect iterators or a std::vector<actionT*> of
    "to-erase" entries during the loop) and actions.remove(...) them after the loop. This makes
    the failure handling consistent with invokeAction (which only erases from the map) and
    removes a surprising side-effect on the caller's function objects.

    Design

    5. Fix invalid_action — change private inheritance to public, remove the public std::string
    what data member, and instead store the message and override const char* what() const
    noexcept override. Keeps catch (const std::exception&) working and removes the
    name-shadowing of what.
    6. Finish the SFINAE → if constexpr migration — rewrite invokeAction body to mirror
    operator(): use if constexpr (std::is_same_v<typename actionT::result_type, void>) directly.
     Then delete both select_actuate overloads (the non-void one's bogus default-constructed
    return goes with it).
    7. Drop std::move on the local in both connect overloads — change return
    std::move(actuator); to return actuator; to re-enable NRVO.
    8. Add const to observers — type(), is_connected(), has_action() become const. Make
    has_action take const std::string& instead of std::string by value.

    Minor

    9. Fix doc comment on mapActions — change //!< Actions list. to //!< Named actions map.
    (actuator.hpp:71).
    10. Remove mutable from both bind lambdas — nothing in either body mutates a capture;
    mutable was dead.
    11. Avoid the std::vector<int> results allocation for void actuators — specialize resultsT
    so void result types yield an empty/no-op type, e.g. using resultsT =
    std::conditional_t<std::is_void_v<...>, std::tuple<>, std::vector<typename
    actionT::result_type>>;. Lower priority — only worth doing if the partial-migration cleanup
    in (6) makes it natural.
    12. Make bind(classT*, method) runtime-safe — inside the lambda, if (obj == nullptr) throw
    invalid_action("bind::method: invalid object"); so its failure mode matches the shared_ptr
    overload instead of relying on a debug-only assert.

    Suggested commit order

    13. Land bugs first as one commit — items 1–4 are short, isolated, and each has (or will
    have) a test. Tests already in actuator_test.cpp should still pass; add a new
    TEST(test_actuator, test_self_assignment) and a TEST(test_actuator,
    test_bind_temporary_shared_ptr) to lock in 2 and 3.
    14. Land design changes as a second commit — items 5–8 together are a coherent API/cleanup
    pass and don't change behavior in ways the existing tests should catch. Run the full suite
    after.
    15. Land minor cleanups as a third commit — items 9–12. Item 11 may want benchmarking
    against performance_test.cpp before/after; if it's noise, skip.